# ringbuf

A tiny fixed-size ring buffer for byte streams, written in plain C99 with no allocations after init.

## Usage

```c
#include "ringbuf.h"

static uint8_t storage[256];
static ringbuf_t rb;

int main(void) {
    ringbuf_init(&rb, storage, sizeof storage);
    ringbuf_push(&rb, 0x41);
    ringbuf_push(&rb, 0x42);

    uint8_t out;
    while (ringbuf_pop(&rb, &out) == RB_OK) {
        putchar(out);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

## Return codes

| Code | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| RB_OK | operation succeeded |
| RB_FULL | push attempted on a full buffer |
| RB_EMPTY | pop attempted on an empty buffer |

## Notes

The capacity is always the size of the backing array minus one, because one slot is kept free to tell a full buffer from an empty one. Push and pop are safe between one producer and one consumer if the indices are declared volatile on your platform.

